      "narrative": "You lean across the scarred table toward Mara Venn. The copper lamps hiss softly against the morning rain drumming the shutters. She doesn't flinch from your gaze \u2014 just holds it, one finger tapping a slow rhythm on her clay mug.\n\n\"The Ash Gate,\" she says, and the name lands heavy in the air between you. \"It's been sealed for seventeen years. Not locked. Sealed.\"\n\nShe reaches into her coat and pulls out a folded scrap of parchment, yellowed at the edges, and slides it across to you. On it, a sketch of the gate's lock mechanism \u2014 intricate, seven pins deep, with a central tumbler shaped like nothing you've ever seen.\n\n\"Toran Vex was the lockwright who maintained it,\" she says, nodding toward the nervous clerk at the bar. \"He's the one who told me the gate didn't just stop working. It was *stopped*.\"\n\nShe leans closer. Her voice drops.\n\n\"Three nights before it sealed, Old Bram \u2014 the ferryman \u2014 came stumbling into this very tavern. Said he'd seen something move beyond the gate. Something that *watched back*. Next morning, they found him slumped against the arch, stone dead. No wound. No cause. Just... empty.\"\n\nShe lets that hang.\n\n\"Town council locked it the same day. Hired masons to wall the archway shut. But the wall didn't take. Mortar crumbled overnight. Stone cracked. They tried three times before giving up and just... leaving it.\"\n\nShe taps the sketch. \"That lock isn't meant to keep people *out*, Kael. It's meant to keep something *in*.\"\n\nShe sits back, watching you process. Toran glances over from the bar, ink-stained fingers fidgeting with a quill, and quickly looks away.\n\n\"So.\" Mara's voice is quiet. \"What do you make of that?\""
    },
