      "narrative": "Mara's fingers curl around her mug as she holds your gaze. The rain drums against the shutters, a steady pulse behind the silence.\n\n\"That's the thing,\" she says, quieter now. \"Officially? Nobody sold passage. The Ash Gate was a public thoroughfare \u2014 no toll, no keeper's fee, no ledger. Town council paid Old Bram a stipend to maintain the lock and run the ferry on the other side. They called him a *custodian*, not a merchant.\"\n\nShe takes a slow drink, then sets the mug down.\n\n\"Unofficially? Bram had a side arrangement. He'd waive the ferry fee for travelers who brought him *things* \u2014 information, old documents, strange trinkets. Nothing that ever showed in a town record. But I know because I tracked one of those transactions to its source.\"\n\nShe taps the sketch.\n\n\"Three months before Sable vanished, a man named **Corvin Ashford** paid Bram a pouch of silver to cross with a sealed iron box. Corvin was a scribe from the Sable Quill \u2014 the courier service Sable herself ran. He carried messages for her. When I asked him about that crossing, he told me the box was addressed to someone on the other side. Someone he'd never met. Just a name on a wax seal.\"\n\nShe pauses.\n\n\"Corvin Ashford died of a fever six weeks after Bram. No connection anyone could prove. But he's the only name I ever found tied to a paid crossing.\"\n\nShe watches you, waiting to see if the name means anything."
    },
