      "narrative": "The Copper Finch's door groans shut behind you, cutting the tavern's warmth. Morning rain hits your face \u2014 thin and cold, washing the last of the lamp-smoke from your clothes.\n\nThe street curves north through a district of shuttered shops and empty stalls. Mud sucks at your boots. A rat bolts from a broken barrel and vanishes into a drain. No one else is about. The rain has scrubbed the town quiet.\n\nFifty paces. A hundred. The buildings thin. The cobbles give way to packed earth.\n\nAnd then you see it.\n\nThe Ash Gate rises against the grey sky \u2014 a cracked black arch, twice the height of a man, standing alone in a patch of churned mud. No wall surrounds it. No fence. Just the arch, stark and wrong, like a doorframe built for a house that was never finished.\n\nA figure stands beside it.\n\nA travel-stained cloak hangs heavy with rain. A courier's satchel is slung across their chest. They're facing the gate, not moving, just *looking* at the dark space within the arch \u2014 as if reading something written there.\n\nThey haven't noticed you yet."
