      "narrative": "The name leaves your mouth and hangs in the damp air, swallowed by the rain's steady hiss.\n\nNo answer.\n\nThe gate's arch stands hollow and dark \u2014 not a tunnel of shadow, but a flat, depthless black that seems to absorb the grey morning light rather than give way to it. The silence that follows your call feels heavier than it should. Thicker. Like the gate is listening too.\n\nThe courier by the arch turns.\n\nA woman's face, sharp-boned and rain-streaked, peels from the shadow of her hood. She looks at you with the flat assessment of someone who's spent years reading strangers at a glance.\n\n\"Old Bram's dead,\" she says. Voice low, worn thin by weather and road dust. \"Has been for seventeen years. You're not the first to call his name at this gate. You won't be the last.\"\n\nShe doesn't move. Doesn't threaten. Just watches you from beneath the dripping brim of her hood, one hand resting loose on her satchel strap."
