      "narrative": "You drop to a crouch beneath the arch, eyes sweeping the churned mud and scattered pebbles. Your fingers brush the damp earth. Nothing. Just rain-washed stone and the grit of seventeen years of weather. No disturbed soil. No hidden seams. No scrape marks suggesting something was buried or retrieved.\n\nThe courier's boots shift in the mud. A soft, wet sound.\n\n\"You're asking the ground questions it won't answer.\" Her tone carries no mockery \u2014 just flat certainty. She gestures with her chin toward the gate's dark interior. \"That arch doesn't hide things in the dirt. It hides them *through* itself. Whatever's hidden here, it's not under your feet.\"\n\nShe adjusts her satchel strap, rain dripping from her cloak's edge.\n\n\"You want to know what's hidden? You'll need to ask the lock. Or the person who built it.\""
